We followed Minar to the crest of the rise, 
where we could see smoke rising from behind a 
smaller hill that lay in front of us.

It was a short walk to the next hill; before us 
sat a ramshackle hut, its battered door flapping
slowly in the breeze. It was hard to believe 
that one could live in such a place, but the 
column of smoke rising from the chimney 
convinced us otherwise."The hut was not here 
last year," he said, "I am at a loss to 
understand it."

"Most peculiar!" said Praxix. "Whom do you 
suppose would choose to live here?"
