"B'ran agrith," I said in my best imitation of 
an Elvish accent. The Elf paused for a moment, 
then smiled. You can imagine my surprise when 
she spoke to me in my own tongue.

"Do not be so surprised," she said. "There are 
many enemies abroad on our lands, and many would
appear to be friends. Some, indeed, speak our 
tongue to deceive us. But it is plain that you 
have met Agrith, and he has always been like kin
to the Elves. But more of this later. We had 
best look after your friend. I believe I can 
help if you will take me to him."

With little time to lose, we sped through the 
forest, finally reaching our camp at sunset.
[wounded]  

We arrived a short time later; Minar appeared 
lifeless, though he was breathing slowly. The 
Wood Elf strode to his side, placed her spindled
arm on his wound, and entered a trance-like 
state.

Just then, from out of nowhere, Praxix rose from
the ground! I was mesmerized by the sight, and 
stood there, speechless. "Don't just stand 
there!" he shouted, "Help an old man up!"

Embarrassed, I helped him out of a hollowed-out 
stump that had been inconspicuously sitting 
nearby. But before I could ask him how he had 
gotten there, he rushed toward Minar, then 
stopped, startled by the sight of my new friend.

"You have done well, Tag! Very well indeed!" I 
blushed, for it wasn't often this Wizard handed 
out compliments. "There are but few Wood Elves 
left in this world, and lucky is the day we meet
one!"

We approached Minar and watched in silence as 
the Wood Elf healed him with her touch. It took 
a long while, though it was still light when 
Minar's eyes regained their sight and his body 
stirred. "What are you all looking at?" he said,
blankly, and even Praxix' lips turned up into a 
smile.

Bergon, who had missed all of the excitement, 
returned a short time later, and, relieved at 
Minar's condition, told of his adventures.

I thought this might be a good time for 
introductions, so I presented the Wood Elf to 
the others. "I am happy to introduce..." I 
started, but came up empty, and the Elf placed 
her hand on my shoulder. "V'ren, of the House of
G'lor," she said. "And, if you wish, I shall 
take you to the Elf Home."

We thanked V'ren for her gracious offer of help,
but we had much to consider before we could 
commit to any decision. V'ren stood and waited 
as we discussed our options.
