Early the next morning, we started again. The 
air was heavy with mist, and it was difficult to
see just a few paces before us. We had been 
moving silently for an hour when Bergon became 
alarmed.

Gathering together, we soon learned that Praxix 
was missing from our ranks. "Praxix! Praxix!" we
called, but there was no reply, only the sounds 
of the forest.

"Praxix! Praxix!" The words fell only upon the 
deaf ears of the forest.

***

"Praxix! Praxix!" the voice cried out. "Wake 
up!"

Praxix rose slowly and took stock of his 
situation. After separating from the group, he 
had wandered aimlessly through the dense fog for
the better part of the morning before stopping 
to indulge in the tasty, red berries that 
blanketed the ground beneath him. And then, he 
fell into a deep, yet restless, sleep.

Now, someone was calling him, but who? He swung 
around, but found himself alone. His head ached,
and he wished for nothing more than to be left 
in peace until the pain subsided.

"Praxix! Don't you have anything better to do," 
the voice continued, "than to sleep the day 
away?"

Praxix swung around again and faced a 
modest-sized oak tree as it was just finishing 
the preceding question. "You know my name?" he 
asked, startled more by the personal reference 
than the fact that he was being addressed in 
whatever fashion by the local flora.

"Why, of course!" the tree replied 
enthusiastically. "All trees sing your praises."

"They do?" Praxix responded coyly, for he was 
not above flattery.

"Oh, my, yes! We know how you helped old Tully 
when his house caught fire. And then there was 
the poor Helspar widow, how you helped her..."

"Yes, yes, I see," Praxix interrupted, not 
wishing to pursue the matter further. But he did
not see, at least very clearly, and his head 
throbbed in much the same way that he expected 
the Tremor spell would cause if cast upon his 
cranium.
