The fog had now completely cleared, and we could
see no trace of the dark shadow. We pressed 
onward, all the time aware that dark and ominous
clouds had been building above us all afternoon.
Rain threatened at any moment, and then it came,
heralded by the booming of thunder and the 
pungent smell of a nearby lightning strike. 
Soon, we were in the midst of a downpour, and we
hustled for cover into a nearby shallow cave.

"We should be safe enough here," Bergon said, 
removing his flooded boots.

"Yes, well, safe enough, I suppose," a voice 
that was not one of our own replied. "What I 
mean is, we're safer than out there, but as to 
whether or not we are truly safe...." He paused 
in his monologue, interrupted by some passing 
thought, and then attempted to finish his 
sentence. "Now what was I saying?" he said.

From within our ranks came an unmistakable 
groan, and this brought a cry of recognition 
from the stranger.

"Ah, Praxix," he said, pulling back his cloak 
and moving toward us from the dark recesses of 
the cave. "Imagine that! We meet yet again!"

It was Umber, and he was at his most cheerful, 
happily telling of his exploits since we had 
last met. About an hour into his story, the 
storm abated and the sky brightened, but Umber 
did not seem to notice, or in any event, gave no
indication of a desire to leave the cave.
 
 
 
 
