Pulling the spyglass from my pack, I held it out
to the proprietor who examined it with keen 
interest.

"Quite a rare piece," he said, expressing little
of the enthusiasm we could read in his eyes. 
Oddly, he was still reluctant to make the trade,
although the spyglass was many times more 
valuable than the stone possibly could have 
been. But then, out of greed, perhaps, he 
relented. "The gray stone for the spyglass," he 
said. "Now, if you don't mind, it's closing 
time."

After handing Praxix the gray stone, he ushered 
us back onto the street, where he bade us a 
perfunctory farewell with a wave of his hand.

"I did not realize you had taken the spyglass," 
Praxix said to me as he crumbled the gray stone 
into a fine powder, "You are indeed a fine one; 
full of surprises! I'm certain nothing else 
would have satisfied him."

"I, for one, do not trust him," Bergon said, 
discreetly glancing back toward the shop. "See 
how he peers at us still through his darkened 
window!"
