It was time for dinner, and it had been a long 
time since we had sat down to a meal.

"Shall we?" I asked, bowing and motioning to the
entrance.

"How can we refuse?" said Bergon, smiling 
widely. "I believe we've earned ourselves a fine
meal or two these past weeks."

The tavern was a large and noisy place, with a 
few dozen tables and a large bar. Either the 
drinks were exceptionally good or the food 
exceptionally bad, for the bar was filled with 
noisy sailors and townsfolk while only one table
was occupied, and nobody there was eating.

Pleasantly surprised that our arrival had not 
attracted undue attention, we sat ourselves down
for dinner, with the expectation of checking out
the wharf later that evening.
